<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ahmadnagar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1494">1494</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ahmadnagar is a city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a>, western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, founded in 1494 by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-nizam-shah-i">Ahmad Nizam Shah I</a> as the capital of the Nizam Shahi sultanate. Its fort was defended by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chand-bibi">Chand Bibi</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> in 1595 and fell to them in 1600. The city later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> and in 1803 to the British, who used its fort as a prison.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahmadnagar.jpg" alt="Ahmadnagar" width="320" height="118" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rohit8596 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ahmednagarrailwaystation.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its five successor states, the sultanates of Bijapur, <b>Ahmadnagar</b>, Golconda, Berar and Bidar, carried on its institutions, Persianate court culture and wars with Vijayanagara, and three of them survived until the Mughal conquests of the 1630s to 1680s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its early decades were spent fighting the neighbouring Deccan sultanates of <b>Ahmadnagar</b>, Golconda and Bidar, and the Vijayanagara Empire to the south over the fertile Raichur doab between the Krishna and Tungabhadra rivers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bijapur-sultanate">Bijapur Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Its origins are closely tied to the political turmoil of the western Deccan, where the sultanates of <b>Ahmadnagar</b> and Bijapur were declining under Mughal pressure, and to the rise of local warrior and landholding families known as Marathas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/maratha-empire">Maratha Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The decisive defeat came on 23 January 1565 at the Battle of Talikota, when the combined armies of Bijapur, <b>Ahmadnagar</b>, Golconda and Bidar destroyed the Vijayanagara army and killed its leader Aliya Rama Raya.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1494 he founded the city of <b>Ahmadnagar</b> as his capital, establishing the Nizam Shahi dynasty in the northern Deccan.</blockquote><a href="/state/ahmadnagar-sultanate">Ahmadnagar Sultanate</a></li></ul></section>
